  "body": {
    "id": "thm:sparse-beyond-birthday",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every array in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\) with \\(\\rho=0\\), equivalently \\(N_n/n\\to0\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}{\\sigma_n^2}\\xrightarrow{p}1.\n\\]\nNo condition \\(N_n^2/n\\to0\\) is imposed.  In particular, the conclusion applies whenever the remaining defining conditions of \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\) hold with \\(N_n=M\\lfloor n^{3/4}/M\\rfloor\\); these grouped-unit counts satisfy \\(N_n/n\\to0\\) but \\(N_n^2/n\\to\\infty\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "M",
      "N_n",
      "\\rho",
      "\\sigma_n^2",
      "\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}",
      "\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:sampling-fraction",
      "ass:scaled-variance-nondegeneracy",
      "def:dense-correction",
      "def:cr2-statistic",
      "def:dense-schedule-class",
      "thm:dense-projection-limit",
      "thm:cr2-phase-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Assume \\(\\rho=0\\).  By Assumption~\\ref{ass:sampling-fraction}, this is equivalent to \\(N_n/n\\to0\\).  Theorem~\\ref{thm:dense-projection-limit} gives the unconditional variance expansion\n\\[\n G_n\\sigma_n^2=R_n+o(1),\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nbecause the degree-one correction is multiplied by \\(\\rho\\).  Independently, Theorem~\\ref{thm:cr2-phase-frontier} gives\n\\[\n G_n\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}=R_n+o_p(1).\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nScaled-variance nondegeneracy, which is one of the conditions in Definition~\\ref{def:dense-schedule-class}, states\n\\[\n \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}G_n\\sigma_n^2\\ge c_\\sigma>0.\n\\]\nThus the deterministic denominator \\(G_n\\sigma_n^2\\) is bounded away from zero for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Subtracting (1) from (2) and dividing by that denominator yields\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}{\\sigma_n^2}\n =\\frac{G_n\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}{G_n\\sigma_n^2}\n \\xrightarrow{p}1.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThis proof used only the vanishing sampling fraction \\(N_n/n\\to0\\), not the stronger birthday-scale condition \\(N_n^2/n\\to0\\).  To verify that the distinction is nonempty, take\n\\[\n G_n=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n^{3/4}}M\\right\\rfloor,\n \\qquad\n N_n=MG_n,\n\\]\nand choose integers \\(G_{1n}\\) with \\(G_{1n}/G_n\\to p\\in(0,1)\\).  Then, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), the design constraints \\(G_n\\to\\infty\\), \\(N_n=MG_n\\le n\\), and \\(1\\le G_{1n}\\le G_n-1\\) hold, while\n\\[\n \\frac{N_n}{n}=n^{-1/4}(1+o(1))\\to0,\n \\qquad\n \\frac{N_n^2}{n}=n^{1/2}(1+o(1))\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nTherefore every schedule array with these counts that satisfies the remaining conditions of \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\) is covered by (3), even though whole-tuple birthday sparsity fails.  No Gaussian or Wald conclusion is asserted.",
    "justification": "Scalar PAME inference needs a vanishing sampling fraction, not a vanishing whole-tuple collision probability.",
    "gap": "FuSamiiWang2026 imposes the birthday-scale restriction \\(N_n^2/n\\to0\\) because its proof couples the entire group tuple, while this theorem targets only the scalar statistic.",
    "consumer": "Experiments grouping \\(n^{3/4}\\) of \\(n\\) eligible units obtain justified CR2 inference despite failure of whole-design coupling.",
